 with the swastika well it's  because it's a zed it's taken on a  rather sort of spiky meaning but i mean  remember in d-day in 1944 that all the  allied uh equipment was painted with  stripes and they were the invasion  stripes so that anything with stripes on  was allied and that was important  okay well another question here uh  relates to the peace talks we don't hear  too much about them at the moment  between russia and ukraine and what it  would mean for the future of ukraine and  we've heard this from president  zielinski during the course of this war  what is neutral ukraine what does it  mean for ukraine in the long term  president zielenski's made it clear that  this issue of nato membership perhaps  not front and center anymore no it's  something to talk about  it would get it would buy him some time  to talk about neutrality and there are  different models i mean you know in  europe switzerland is is neutral austria  is neutral finland and sweden are  officially neutral until they join nato  fairly soon um so and the austrian state  treaty of 1955 was a treaty that the  soviets agreed to to they withdrew from  austria on condition that neutrality was  built into its constitution so that  provides a model it's something to talk  about and the future future ukraine  would certainly not be demilitarized  because it's it's going to be armed to  the teeth in the future because russia  under putin will always try to re-invade  that's the assumption you have to make  but it could be neutral in terms of its  formal relationships with military  alliances its relationship with nato  that's relatively easy to concede  actually but but for zolensky i think  neutrality is something to talk about  to show what a reasonable leader he is  against this  man in the kremlin who seems crazed by  the things he's saying and what about  the peace talks themselves as i say we  don't hear much about them at the moment  are  our contacts at least still continuing  yeah i mean they talk on a daily basis i  mean the teams are always talking to  each other and there are lots of teams  in in europe who are trying to keep all  the levels open so there's no lack of  there's no lack of contact at a very low  level but they won't talk until they  think they've got something to talk  about and what zelensky has said is look  i will talk when you get out of my  country and given that he's not losing  he's entitled to say that and the west  will back him  in whatever he says if he says i will  talk the west will say fine you talk and  we'll we'll back your judgment if you're  not prepared to talk because you're  you're going to keep on fighting to try  to throw them out of your country we'll  back that as well that's the view that  the west has taken okay we're getting  towards the end of this hour and uh  let's try and reach  some kind of  well conclusion or get towards  some idea of how this might all be  settled david is asking whether russia  was ever offered nato membership and  could they be offered this in the future  and what would it mean start with the  first part for us then michael was  russia ever offered nato membership  during that period of eastwood expansion  no um there was a time when they they  they themselves contemplated it george  robertson who of course was our defense  secretary in britain became the nato  secretary general and he records that in  the early 90s he went to see putin and  this was when um uh sorry the early  2000's putin become uh prime minister  under yeltsin then president after 99  with the background of the ruthless  citizen chechnya and putin said so i  suppose we could be in nato now then he  said all cosy and robertson said well um  you know we don't just take people in i  mean you could apply for membership and  we'd consider it and putin got very  upset and offended and he said you know  russia doesn't take its place in a queue  behind other countries you should want  us in and that was as far as the  conversation went and since then it's  gone out of the window what about this  uh very well presumably very far in the  future and it's got to be a post-putin  future i mean could russia be offered  nato membership it's very hard to  imagine at the moment i mean because  we're now in cold war too we can't get  away from that there's a fundamental  reality about this is a geopolitical  reality of europe and it is that  in europe europe can either be dominated  by germany  by russia or by america and all  europeans need to think about that and  ask themselves germany  russia or america so who would you  prefer  that's it that  that in your assessment so what has  europe has chosen at the moment well  germany and the united states some sort  of combination is basically you're not  you've been in the united states for a  long time but you know britain has never  dominated europe spain did at one time  france did at one time they'll never do  that again um italy did a very long time  ago or the renaissance states but just  think of europe in the period in of  course the last four or 500 years it is  dominated by one of the big powers and  the big powers since the beginning of  the 20th century have been russia  america and germany so it's one of those  three  they have to have predominant influence  and i say to my european friends so who  would you prefer  michael we are out of time thank you so  much geo philosophical question to  conjure with there  at the end thank you once again michael  clark there and you've been watching a  sky news tonight a special program uh on  ukraine coming up at eight the latest  reaction to the news that the prime  minister and the chancellor have been  fined for breaching covet rules  [Music]
 

